# The Conservatives Have a Problem Reform Cannot Solve

**Kemi Badenoch has found a philosophy. The question is whether anyone still believes the Conservative Party can deliver it**

Kemi Badenoch's most revealing words at this year's Conservative Party conference were not about immigration, inheritance tax or even Brexit. They were these: **“This party has learned the lessons.”**

That is an extraordinary claim for a party which spent 14 years in government, lost its voters in 2024, suffered historic local-election defeats and has since watched Reform UK occupy much of the political territory it once regarded as its own. Yet it is also the essential argument of Badenoch's leadership.

Her answer is not to become Nigel Farage. Indeed, her conference performance suggested that she understands precisely why that would be a mistake. Reform can say things about immigration, national sovereignty and the failures of the political establishment with a directness that is much harder for a party with a governing record. The Conservatives cannot simply borrow that language. Their opponents can always reply: **you were in charge. What did you do about it?**

Badenoch's answer is instead to change what the Conservative Party thinks it is for.

Her new document, *The Right Way*, is explicitly modelled on the Conservatives' *Right Approach* of 1976. Its central proposition is unusually stark: Britain's problem is not that government does too little, but that it has **“taken responsibility for too much and forgotten how to deliver.”** The principles are equally clear: wealth must be created before it can be distributed; freedom requires responsibility; rules need consequences; governments should be judged by what they achieve; and the state should be strong where only the state can act but restrained elsewhere.

That is a real intellectual programme.

Badenoch's conference speech reinforced it. She spoke of fixing *causes*, rather than symptoms. She argued that Conservatives had previously failed because they attempted to govern without first establishing what they believed. She promised a party with “strong roots” capable of resisting the pressure of events and short-term political interests.

There is something almost old-fashioned about this. It is an attempt to rebuild a political party around a theory of government rather than a collection of policies.

And yet the Conservative problem is not primarily intellectual.

It is **credibility**.

YouGov's large 2026 study of former Conservative voters found that 26% of the party's 2024 voters had subsequently switched to Reform. A further 10% were undecided. Among those who had left the Conservatives, 24% cited uncertainty that the party would keep its promises, while 11% said they no longer knew what the party stood for.

That is the wound Badenoch is trying to close.

Her conference rhetoric therefore contained an interesting mixture of renewal and retrospective confession. She said Conservatives had previously allowed people to use the party merely to get elected and then betray its values. She promised that candidates would commit themselves to “integrity, duty, discipline” and insisted that voters should know that a Conservative vote means getting a Conservative government.

But here is the awkward part.

**Reform does not have to prove that it is the old Conservative Party.**

That is its great political advantage.

Nigel Farage can look at the accumulated failures of British government and say that the system is broken. At Reform's September conference he promised that a Reform government would stop the boats within 100 days, cut energy bills, expand domestic medical training and reduce taxes on working people and pensioners. Farage then added something even more important: ministers would have 100 days to deliver their pledges or resign.

This is political communication of a different sort.

The Conservatives say: **we have learned the lessons.**

Reform says: **give us 100 days.**

The Conservatives say government should have consequences.

Reform says ministers themselves will have consequences.

The Conservatives say rules need enforcement.

Reform says it will stop the boats.

The Conservatives say wealth creation must precede distribution.

Reform says it will raise the personal allowance to £15,000.

One can dispute the feasibility, cost or legality of Reform's proposals. Indeed, there are ample grounds for doing so. Reform itself has recently faced damaging scrutiny over its handling of prospective donations; its own investigation acknowledged weaknesses in its procedures, while police inquiries continue.

But that is not the same as saying the party's political communication is ineffective.

It has discovered something the Conservatives have found difficult to rediscover: **permission to speak as though the old political settlement has failed.**

That permission matters.

The Conservative Party spent much of its time in government managing compromises. Brexit had to be negotiated. The pandemic had to be managed. Energy prices had to be subsidised. Public spending had to be maintained. Taxes rose. Regulation accumulated. Migration remained high. The party's factions fought one another. Prime ministers came and went.

Now, in opposition, the Conservatives are attempting to tell voters that they have diagnosed the disease.

The voters may reasonably ask why the doctors did not notice it while they were running the hospital.

This is why Badenoch's Birmingham speech was simultaneously more substantial than Reform's and, politically, potentially harder to sell.

Her strongest argument is not that Britain needs another dose of populism. It is that Britain needs **competent national renewal**.

Her Birmingham setting was deliberate. She chose a city whose wealth was built by entrepreneurs, family firms and industrial enterprise, arguing that Birmingham's prosperity came from ordinary people's work rather than central government planning. She presented the Conservatives as a party of the future rather than one attempting to drag Britain backwards.

There is the outline here of something larger than conventional small-government conservatism.

Call it **a one-nation commonwealth**.

The phrase need not mean a return to paternalistic Toryism. It could mean a country in which markets create wealth, families and communities retain responsibility, government concentrates on things only government can do, and the state becomes capable enough to build infrastructure, defend the country, enforce borders and maintain the basic conditions for prosperity.

That would actually distinguish Badenoch from Reform.

Reform's proposition is essentially one of **rupture**: Britain has been badly governed; break the machinery that produced the failure; impose deadlines; reverse policies; restore control.

Badenoch's proposition is potentially one of **reconstruction**: Britain has accumulated dysfunctional institutions; understand why they failed; restore the proper limits of government; rebuild state capacity; then allow society and markets to generate the prosperity government cannot manufacture.

The difficulty is that reconstruction is harder to communicate than rupture.

“Stop the boats” fits on a poster.

“Government should be strong where only the state can act and restrained where people are better placed to decide for themselves” is a governing philosophy.

One is a slogan. The other is a constitution for government.

And this is where the Conservative Party's tarnished reputation becomes decisive. YouGov's research suggests that its former voters are not simply demanding more right-wing policies. Many are questioning whether the party can be trusted to carry out what it says, and whether it has a sufficiently clear identity.

Badenoch has correctly identified the second problem.

She now has to overcome the first.

Nor should Conservatives comfort themselves by assuming Reform has solved the problem merely by speaking more bluntly. Reform's own public ratings have deteriorated: in YouGov's July tracker, 65% viewed the party unfavourably and 27% favourably, while 56% said they would not like Reform to form part of a future government. Opinium likewise found only 25% saying Reform was ready for government, against 54% who disagreed.

Reform, in other words, has a **language advantage**, not necessarily an institutional one.

The Conservatives have almost the opposite problem.

They possess an institutional memory of government but have spent years exhausting the credibility that comes with it.

Badenoch's task is therefore unusually difficult. She must convince voters that the party responsible for much of the recent governing settlement is nevertheless capable of breaking with it.

That is why *The Right Way* may matter more than the conference's individual tax announcements. Badenoch is attempting to make the Conservatives say something that Reform cannot easily say:

**Britain does not merely need to revolt against its political class. It needs to be governed differently.**

That is a much harder proposition to prove.

But it is also potentially a much more consequential one.

The future contest on the British right may therefore not ultimately be between the Conservatives and Reform over who can sound angrier about Britain's problems. It may be between **rupture and reconstruction**.

Reform has learnt to say what the Conservatives could not say while they were in government.

Badenoch is attempting to build a Conservative Party capable of saying what Reform cannot easily say in opposition:

**Here is not merely what is wrong with Britain. Here is how a modern British state should work.**

Whether voters are prepared to believe that the Conservatives can actually build it is the question that will define Badenoch's project.











# The Professional Politician

**Robert Jenrick and the strange career of the political class**

There is something almost too perfect about Robert Jenrick joining Reform.

Richard Katz and Peter Mair's famous “cartel party” thesis, first published in 1995, described a transformation in which established parties became increasingly dependent upon the state, professionalised their organisations and converged in ways that insulated them from the society they purported to represent. The party, in their formulation, increasingly became an agent of the state rather than an intermediary between citizens and it.

Thirty years later, Mr Jenrick presents an intriguing complication.

For if the Conservatives are a cartel party, Mr Jenrick is almost an ideal **professional politician**: lawyer, minister, frontbencher, leadership candidate, media performer and parliamentary operator. He understood Westminster because Westminster was his profession. And yet in January he crossed the floor to Reform, after Kemi Badenoch dismissed him, denouncing the Conservatives as “rotten” and saying that the party “hasn't changed, it won't change, it can't change”.

This is not merely another Conservative defection.

It is a small parable about the condition of the British political class.

### The politician who escaped the cartel

Katz and Mair never argued that politicians were necessarily conspiring in a literal smoke-filled room. Their argument was subtler. Parties that had once depended heavily upon membership and civil society could increasingly rely upon the resources of the state, professional staff and public institutions. Competition did not disappear. But the established competitors acquired common interests in maintaining the political system from which they benefited.

The striking thing about modern British politics is what happens when somebody from inside that system decides to attack it.

Mr Jenrick has not become an outsider.

He has become an **insider who has changed sides**.

That distinction matters.

He brings to Reform precisely those qualities which professional politics rewards: fluency in television studios, knowledge of parliamentary procedure, familiarity with the press, policy vocabulary, ministerial experience and an instinct for political positioning. When he arrived at Reform, he was not recruited from the street. He was imported from Westminster.

The insurgency is acquiring professionals.

That is perhaps the most revealing development of all.

### From Cameron to Farage

Mr Jenrick's career makes the transition especially conspicuous. He began political life within the modern Conservative establishment and subsequently moved towards increasingly restrictive positions on immigration and the state. By 2026 he had crossed into the organisation that most explicitly presents itself as the enemy of that establishment. Sky News has described the trajectory from Cameron-era Conservative to anti-immigration campaigner and finally Reform politician.

The obvious interpretation is hypocrisy: the professional politician follows the political market.

There is another interpretation.

Perhaps professional politicians are unusually well placed to see the weaknesses of the system in which they operate.

Mr Jenrick's January denunciation was remarkable because it contained the accusation that politicians around the Conservative shadow cabinet table privately knew Britain was “broken” but could not admit it because “it was us who broke it”.

That is almost a textbook anti-cartel argument.

The established party is supposedly unable to tell the truth about the system because it is implicated in the system.

Reform, by contrast, can say it.

### The great advantage of Reform

This gives Reform something the Conservatives cannot manufacture through communications consultants: **political distance**.

A Conservative minister saying that Westminster is dysfunctional is immediately confronted with the question: *you were Westminster*.

A Reform politician saying the same thing can point across the chamber.

This explains some of the peculiar attraction of Reform's rhetoric. It is not necessarily that every Reform proposition is novel. Many are recognisably Conservative policies in more emphatic form. The difference is the speaker's relationship to the governing establishment.

Reform can say that the political class failed.

The Conservatives have to say that the political class failed **and include themselves in the indictment**.

Badenoch has plainly understood this problem. Her response has been to tell defectors that the Conservatives are changing while simultaneously portraying Reform as an aggregation of former colleagues, “drama queens” and political opportunists. At the 2026 conference she pointed out that some former Conservatives who had once been “measuring up the curtains for Number 10” were now facing serious controversy and scrutiny.

It is a clever attack.

It is also an unusually revealing one.

The Conservative leader's argument is essentially that **Reform is not anti-establishment enough**.

Its politicians came from the establishment.

### The professional politician comes home

And here the Katz-Mair thesis needs updating.

The original cartel model was principally concerned with established parties becoming increasingly embedded in the state and increasingly detached from civil society. But the newer literature suggests that the story does not end there. A 2026 reassessment by Nick Martin and André Krouwel describes the subsequent phenomenon as a movement from “cartel to crusade”: established-party cartelisation can generate radical outbidding by challengers seeking to break the political equilibrium.

Britain offers an almost laboratory-like example.

The old parties professionalised.

The insurgent parties attacked professionalisation.

Then the professionals started joining the insurgents.

Mr Jenrick therefore represents something more interesting than an ordinary defection. **He is the cartel's product becoming the cartel's critic.**

And that should make the Conservatives rather more worried than another opinion poll.

### But Reform has a problem too

There is an irony here.

If Reform fills itself with professional politicians, it may eventually acquire the characteristics it was founded to oppose.

This is the historical trap of insurgent parties.

They begin as movements.

They acquire MPs.

They acquire advisers.

They acquire donors.

They acquire lawyers.

They acquire policy units.

They acquire media operations.

They acquire parliamentary offices.

Eventually they acquire something still more dangerous: **responsibility**.

Then the question changes from “Why hasn't Westminster fixed Britain?” to “Why haven't you?”

That is when the professional politician becomes useful.

Mr Jenrick knows how Westminster works. He knows how legislation works. He knows how government departments work. He knows how a minister survives a bad headline. He knows how the political machine talks to itself.

Those are precisely the skills an insurgent movement needs if it ever intends to become a governing party.

But they are also the skills that can turn an insurgency into another political machine.

### The cartel's final irony

This leaves British politics in an oddly symmetrical position.

The Conservatives accuse Reform of being a circus.

Reform accuses the Conservatives of being a cartel.

The Conservatives say Reform is full of defectors.

Reform says the Conservatives are full of people who cannot change.

And somewhere between them stands Robert Jenrick: a man sufficiently professional to have risen to the top of the Conservative Party, and sufficiently disillusioned with it to leave for the movement whose central proposition is that the professional political class has failed.

There is a lesson here for both parties.

The Conservatives cannot defeat Reform merely by becoming more respectable.

Nor can Reform defeat the Conservatives merely by becoming less respectable.

The real contest is over whether **professional politics itself can be made useful again**.

Katz and Mair's great insight was that political parties can cease to be vehicles through which society controls the state and instead become institutions that help reproduce the political system itself.

Mr Jenrick's career adds an amusing British epilogue.

Sometimes the professional politician discovers the cartel from the inside.

And sometimes, having discovered it, he crosses the street.


